<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asir</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asir is a mountainous region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, now a province of Saudi Arabia with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abha">Abha</a>. Its highlands catch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> rains and support terraced farming. It joined the first Saudi state in the early 19th century, then was ruled by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the Idrisid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Asir before being absorbed by Saudi Arabia in the 1920s and 1930s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asir.jpg" alt="Asir" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heritage Commission · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B1%D8%AC%D8%A7%D9%84_%D8%A3%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B91.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Asir</b>, the mountainous region between the Hejaz and Yemen, had joined the Emirate of Diriyah and fought hard against the Egyptian armies of Muhammad Ali.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-asir">Emirate of Asir</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi, great-grandson of the Moroccan Sufi teacher Ahmad ibn Idris, settled at Sabya in the coastal Tihama of <b>Asir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/idrisid-emirate-of-asir">Idrisid Emirate of Asir</a></li><li><blockquote>A war with Saudi Arabia in 1934 lost <b>Asir</b>, Jizan and Najran under the Treaty of Ta'if.</blockquote><a href="/state/mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen">Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultanate covered central and eastern Arabia and, from 1920, highland <b>Asir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-nejd">Sultanate of Nejd</a></li></ul></section>
